<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!--
      Cold start on this app is not quick: the WebView has to come up, Rust has
      to open SQLite and run migrations, and a large JavaScript bundle has to
      parse. With no theme of its own the window was plain white for all of it,
      which on a slower phone is several seconds of nothing.

      `windowBackground` is the fix that matters. It paints the app's own ground
      colour from the first frame, so the gap is the app's colour rather than a
      flash of white — and it applies again in every moment the WebView has not
      painted yet, not only at launch.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.synabit" parent="Theme.MaterialComponents.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/app_background</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      The theme the launcher actually starts. Hands over to Theme.synabit once
      the activity is up; MainActivity calls installSplashScreen() to make that
      handover happen at the right moment.

      No animated icon on purpose: an adaptive icon's foreground carries a large
      safe-area margin and renders oversized here, and that is not something to
      guess at without seeing it on a device. A clean colour field is a better
      answer than a badly cropped logo.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.synabit.Starting" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/app_background</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/Theme.synabit</item>
    </style>
</resources>
